# Auto-mount/unmount the dashcam recording USB drive by filesystem LABEL.
#
# Unlike ultima-data-mount.sh's /data (every SD card dd'd from this
# project's own image shares one UUID/LABEL, so that mount is deliberately
# NOT identified that way — see that unit's own comment), this drive is the
# only removable disk on the system, so its own LABEL genuinely,
# unambiguously identifies it. Deliberately matches on LABEL alone, not
# filesystem type — reformatting the labeled drive to something other than
# exFAT still mounts (systemd-mount auto-detects the type); an unlabeled or
# differently-labeled drive is left completely alone, no auto-format.
#
# Numbered 99- so it runs after systemd/udev's own built-in blkid probing
# (the rules that populate ID_FS_LABEL in the first place, typically in the
# 60-series) within the same uevent pass — that ordering is load-bearing,
# not cosmetic: this rule reading ID_FS_LABEL before it's populated would
# just never match.
#
# noatime: avoid touching exFAT metadata on every read (flash wear/write
# amplification — same wear-conscious reasoning as this project's
# read-only-rootfs work). nosuid,nodev,noexec: standard hardening for a
# removable, physically-accessible-in-the-car filesystem — nothing on it
# should ever be treated as executable or a device node.
# The add path goes through ultima-dvr-mount (launched non-blocking as a
# transient unit so a slow fsck never stalls the udev worker): it preen-fscks
# the exFAT filesystem — no journal, and this drive is power-cut constantly —
# then systemd-mounts it with the same hardening options. See
# ultima-dvr-mount.sh and DASHCAM.md M3.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_LABEL}=="ULTIMA_DVR", \
    RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect --unit=ultima-dvr-mount /usr/bin/ultima-dvr-mount $env{DEVNAME}"

ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_LABEL}=="ULTIMA_DVR", \
    RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-umount /mnt/dvr"
